1 ON
    The ON command says what a command procedure does when one of its
    commands fails, or when CTRL/Y is pressed while it runs.  Without
    one, a procedure ends when a command fails with an error or a fatal
    error, and goes on after a warning.

    Syntax:

        ON condition THEN [$] command

    Parameters:

        condition   WARNING         a warning, an error, or a fatal error
                    ERROR           an error or a fatal error
                    SEVERE_ERROR    a fatal error
                    CONTROL_Y       CTRL/Y pressed while the
                                    procedure runs

        command     The command to run in place of the procedure's usual
                    action, such as EXIT, CONTINUE, or SHOW SYMBOL
                    $STATUS.

    The command runs once: after it, the usual action is back until the
    next ON.  ON CONTROL_Y is the exception: its command runs for each
    CTRL/Y until another ON CONTROL_Y, SET NOCONTROL=Y, or the end of
    the procedure.  If an image is running when CTRL/Y is pressed, it
    is stopped first; a command of DCL's own completes first, and the
    procedure goes on after it unless the command (GOTO, EXIT, STOP)
    goes elsewhere.  ON applies only to the command level it is in, so a
    procedure the procedure runs has its own.  SET NOON turns the
    checking off, and SET ON (or an ON) back on.  At the terminal, ON
    is accepted and does nothing.

    Example:

        $ ON WARNING THEN EXIT
        $ ON ERROR THEN CONTINUE
        $ ON CONTROL_Y THEN GOTO CLEAN_UP
